===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|an answer or reply}}An [[answer]] or [[reply]], or something in the nature of an answer or reply.
#: {{syn|en|antiphon|q1=literary}}
#: {{ux|en|When pushed for an answer, she gave the stock '''response'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|the act of responding or replying}}The act of [[respond]]ing or replying; reply: as, to speak in response to a question.
#: {{ux|en|A ready '''response''' to questions about wrongdoing}}
# An [[oracular]] answer.
# {{lb|en|liturgics}} A [[verse]], sentence, phrase, or word said or sung by the [[choir]] or [[congregation]] in sequence or reply to the [[priest]] or [[officiant]].
# {{lb|en|liturgics}} A [[versicle]] or [[anthem]] said or sung during or after a [[lection]]; a [[respond]] or [[responsory]].
# A reply to an [[objection]] in formal [[disputation]].
# An online advertising performance metric representing one [[click-through]] from an online ad to its destination [[URL]].
# A [[reaction]] to a [[stimulus]] or [[provocation]].
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=July-August|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/stephen-p-lownie Stephen P. Lownie]; [http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/david-m-pelz David M. Pelz]|magazine=w:American Scientist|title=Stents to Prevent Stroke|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/stents-to-prevent-stroke|passage=As we age, the major arteries of our bodies frequently become thickened with plaque, a fatty material with an oatmeal-like consistency that builds up along the inner lining of blood vessels. The reason plaque forms isn’t entirely known, but it seems to be related to high levels of cholesterol inducing an inflammatory '''response''', which can also attract and trap more cellular debris over time.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170529010543/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/4/stents-to-prevent-stroke|archivedate=29 May 2017}}

